hitespace-only title — never throws, never silently trims to empty.\n170\t- The store's read and write functions take the YAML file path as an explicit parameter — no hardcoded global path — so tests read and write a real temp file with no mocking.\n171\t- Reading a store path that does not exist yields an empty todo list, not an error.\n172\t- Writing a list of todos to the store and reading it back yields an equal list (round-trip test).\n173\t- Reading a malformed YAML store file fails fast with a descriptive error on the `IO`/`Either` error channel, not a partial result or a raw\n174\t  stack trace.\n175\t- `todo add \"<title>\"` appends one `Todo` with `done = false` to the store and prints confirmation naming the new id.\n176\t- `todo add \"\"` (or an all-whitespace title) leaves the store untouched, exits non-zero, and names the problem.\n177\t- `todo list` on an empty store prints a \"no todos\" message and exits 0.\n178\t- `todo list` after two `add`s prints both todos, each with its id, title and done state.\n179\t\n180\t## M4: scalafmt quality gate as a pre-commit hook (Status: IMPLEMENTED)\n181\t\n182\tStop unformatted code from ever reaching a commit, not just from passing CI after the fact.\n183\t\n184\t**Implementation Details:** The hook infrastructure this milestone\n185\tdescribes — `.githooks/pre-commit` (a `set -euo pipefail` script whose\n186\tonly job is `just check`) and the `install-hooks` recipe in `justfile`\n187\t(`git config core.hooksPath .githooks` then `chmod +x\n188\t.githooks/pre-commit`) — already existed in the working tree before this\n189\tmilestone was implemented, and `CLAUDE.md` already documented running\n190\t`just install-hooks` once per clone under its Checks section. `just\n191\tcheck` itself (`sbt -batch scalafmtCheckAll test`, plus the line/test/warning\n192\tsummary) also predates this milestone. No source changed for M4; the\n193\twork here was verifying, end to end in a disposable clone (`git clone`\n194\tto `/tmp`, so the real repo's hook state was never touched), that the\n195\texisting pieces actually satisfy every acceptance criterion rather than\n196\ttaking the prior commits' intent on faith:\n197\t`just install-hooks` sets `core.hooksPath` and makes the script\n198\texecutable; introducing a scalafmt violation (`val message:String=...`\n199\twith no spaces) and running `git commit` fails with scalafmt's `isn't\n200\tformatted properly!` warning, a non-zero exit from `just check`, and no\n201\tnew commit (`git log` unchanged); running `sbt scalafmtAll` to reformat\n202\tand committing a genuinely different, correctly formatted change then\n203\tsucceeds with no manual `just check` step, running the full check (20\n204\ttests, 0 warnings) as part of the commit itself. This is a\n205\tprocess/tooling criterion rather than a domain-logic one, so its \"test\"\n206\tis the shell transcript above rather than a `munit` suite — there is no\n207\tpure function to unit-test here, only a git/filesystem integration that\n208\t`docs/rules/WOW.md`'s \"no mocks\" rule would have us exercise for real\n209\texactly as done.\n210\t\n211\t**Acceptance Criteria:**\n212\t\n213\t- A versioned git hook script under `.githooks/` runs `just check` (scalafmt-check, compile, test) before a commit is created.\n214\t- `just install-hooks` points git at `.githooks` (`core.hooksPath`) and makes the hook executable, so one command per clone is enough to enable it.\n215\t- Committing a change with a scalafmt violation is rejected by the hook (non-zero exit, commit not created) until the file is reformatted or `sbt scalafmtAll` is run.\n216\t- A correctly formatted change commits cleanly through the hook with no manual `just check` step.\n217\t- `CLAUDE.md` (or equivalent onboarding doc) documents running `just install-hooks` once per clone.\n218\t\n219\t## M5: List as table (Status: PENDING)\n220\t\n221\tTurn the plain listing from M3 into an aligned table.\n222\t\n223\t**Acceptance Criteria:**\n224\t\n225\t- `todo list` output starts with a header row naming the columns, then one row per todo.\n226\t- Columns stay aligned when values differ in width (a 1-digit vs 3-digit id, a short vs long title) — checked by asserting on the rendered string.\n227\t- The done/pending state renders as a distinct marker per row (e.g. `[x]`/`[ ]`), not a raw boolean.\n228\t- The empty-store case still renders the M3 \"no todos\" message, not a header with zero rows.\n229\t- The renderer is a pure function `List[Todo] => String`, tested directly with no I/O.\n230\t\n231\t## M6: Updates (Status: PENDING)\n232\t\n233\tMark todos done, edit their title, and remove them.\n234\t\n235\t**Acceptance Criteria:**\n236\t\n237\t- `todo done <id>` sets that todo's `done` flag and persists it; a subsequent `todo list` shows it as done.\n238\t- `todo done <id>` on an already-done todo is an idempotent no-op that still exits 0.\n239\t- `todo edit <id> \"<title>\"` replaces that todo's title under the same non-empty validation as `add`, and persists the change.\n240\t- `todo remove <id>` deletes that todo; a subsequent `todo list` no longer shows it.\n241\t- `done`, `edit` and `remove` given an id absent from the store each exit non-zero, name the unknown id, and leave the store unchanged (verified by reading the store before and after).\n242\t- Each operation is backed by a pure function over `List[Todo]` (`(List[Todo], ...) => Either[<error>, List[Todo]]`) tested without touching the filesystem; the CLI layer only wires that function to the store read/write.\n243\t\n244\t## M7: Add colors (Status: PENDING)\n245\t\n246\tColorize the table without breaking non-terminal output.\n247\t\n248\t**Acceptance Criteria:**\n249\t\n250\t- On a color-capable terminal, done and pending rows render with visibly different ANSI colors in the output string.\n251\t- Setting `NO_COLOR` (per no-color.org) suppresses all ANSI codes — asserted by checking the rendered string contains no escape sequences.\n252\t- When stdout is not a TTY (e.g. piped to a file), ANSI codes are suppressed by default, same as the `NO_COLOR` case.\n253\t- The color-enabled decision is a pure function of an explicit boolean computed once at the edge (from env and TTY check) — the coloring function itself is tested by passing `true`/`false` directly, no real terminal or env mutation needed.\n254\t- The M5 column-alignment tests still pass with color disabled: stripping ANSI codes from a colored row yields the same text as the uncolored row.\n255\t\n256\t## M8: Add YAML output and a switch argument (Status: PENDING)\n257\t\n258\tLet `list` render as YAML in addition to the colored table, selected by an explicit flag.\n259\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c107c929-b48e-44c9-89f6-008ba03025f5","uuid":"dd1f6d0d-c226-4df9-b437-144eff67c66b","timestamp":"2026-09-30T01:21:04.447Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/isaiasbartelborth/Projects/hivemind/todo-list-cli/develop/docs/specs/SPEC.v1-todo-list-cli-mvp.md","content":"a present file is parsed with `.as[List[TodoRecord]]`, and both a\n`YamlError` (malformed syntax/shape) and an invalid record surface as a\n`StoreError` with a human-readable `.message` — never a partial result,\nnever a raw stack trace. One real bug surfaced by writing the round-trip\ntest *before* the fix (per the TDD requirement): `org.virtuslab::scala-yaml` renders an\nempty `Seq` as the empty string, which then fails to reparse\n(`ComposerError: Expected YAML node, but found: StreamEnd`); `write`\nspecial-cases an empty todo list to the literal `\"[]\\n\"` so an emptied\nstore still round-trips.\n\n`todo.render.Renderer.renderList: List[Todo] => String` is the M3 plain\nlisting (`No todos yet.` when empty, one `#<id> [pending|done] <title>`\nline per todo otherwise) — a pure function with no `IO`, which M5 will\nreplace with a table.\n\n`todo.Cli` wires it together: `Cli.run(args, storePath, out)` takes the\nstore path and output stream as explicit parameters (`Main` supplies the\nreal `todos.yaml` in the working directory and `System.out`; tests\nsupply a temp file and a captured stream), so the CLI is exercised\nend-to-end with a real fixture file and no process spawn or mocking.\n`add` reads the store, calls `Todos.add`, and only writes back on\nsuccess; a blank title never reaches `Store.write`, leaving the store\nuntouched, and exits with `ExitCode.Error` naming the problem. A\nsuccessful `add` writes the updated list and prints\n`Added todo #<id>: <title>`. `list` reads the store and prints\n`Renderer.renderList`.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n\n- `Todo` holds an id, a non-empty title and a `done` flag; the title's smart constructor returns `Either[<error>, Todo]` for a blank or whitespace-only title — never throws, never silently trims to empty.\n- The store's read and write functions take the YAML file path as an explicit parameter — no hardcoded global path — so tests read and write a real temp file with no mocking.\n- Reading a store path that does not exist yields an empty todo list, not an error.\n- Writing a list of todos to the store and reading it back yields an equal list (round-trip test).\n- Reading a malformed YAML store file fails fast with a descriptive error on the `IO`/`Either` error channel, not a partial result or a raw\n  stack trace.\n- `todo add \"<title>\"` appends one `Todo` with `done = false` to the store and prints confirmation naming the new id.\n- `todo add \"\"` (or an all-whitespace title) leaves the store untouched, exits non-zero, and names the problem.\n- `todo list` on an empty store prints a \"no todos\" message and exits 0.\n- `todo list` after two `add`s prints both todos, each with its id, title and done state.\n\n## M4: scalafmt quality gate as a pre-commit hook (Status: IMPLEMENTED)\n\nStop unformatted code from ever reaching a commit, not just from passing CI after the fact.\n\n**Implementation Details:** The hook infrastructure this milestone\ndescribes — `.githooks/pre-commit` (a `set -euo pipefail` script whose\nonly job is `just check`) and the `install-hooks` recipe in `justfile`\n(`git config core.hooksPath .githooks` then `chmod +x\n.githooks/pre-commit`) — already existed in the working tree before this\nmilestone was implemented, and `CLAUDE.md` already documented running\n`just install-hooks` once per clone under its Checks section. `just\ncheck` itself (`sbt -batch scalafmtCheckAll test`, plus the line/test/warning\nsummary) also predates this milestone. No source changed for M4; the\nwork here was verifying, end to end in a disposable clone (`git clone`\nto `/tmp`, so the real repo's hook state was never touched), that the\nexisting pieces actually satisfy every acceptance criterion rather than\ntaking the prior commits' intent on faith:\n`just install-hooks` sets `core.hooksPath` and makes the script\nexecutable; introducing a scalafmt violation (`val message:String=...`\nwith no spaces) and running `git commit` fails with scalafmt's `isn't\nformatted properly!` warning, a non-zero exit from `just check`, and no\nnew commit (`git log` unchanged); running `sbt scalafmtAll` to reformat\nand committing a genuinely different, correctly formatted change then\nsucceeds with no manual `just check` step, running the full check (20\ntests, 0 warnings) as part of the commit itself. This is a\nprocess/tooling criterion rather than a domain-logic one, so its \"test\"\nis the shell transcript above rather than a `munit` suite — there is no\npure function to unit-test here, only a git/filesystem integration that\n`docs/rules/WOW.md`'s \"no mocks\" rule would have us exercise for real\nexactly as done.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n\n- A versioned git hook script under `.githooks/` runs `just check` (scalafmt-check, compile, test) before a commit is created.\n- `just install-hooks` points git at `.githooks` (`core.hooksPath`) and makes the hook executable, so one command per clone is enough to enable it.\n- Committing a change with a scalafmt violation is rejected by the hook (non-zero exit, commit not created) until the file is reformatted or `sbt scalafmtAll` is run.\n- A correctly formatted change commits cleanly through the hook with no manual `just check` step.\n- `CLAUDE.md` (or equivalent onboarding doc) documents running `just install-hooks` once per clone.\n\n## M5: List as table (Status: PENDING)\n\nTurn the plain listing from M3 into an aligned table.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n\n- `todo list` output starts with a header row naming the columns, then one row per todo.\n- Columns stay aligned when values differ in width (a 1-digit vs 3-digit id, a short vs long title) — checked by asserting on the rendered string.\n- The done/pending state renders as a distinct marker per row (e.g. `[x]`/`[ ]`), not a raw boolean.\n- The empty-store case still renders the M3 \"no todos\" message, not a header with zero rows.\n- The renderer is a pure function `List[Todo] => String`, tested directly with no I/O.\n\n## M6: Updates (Status: PENDING)\n\nMark todos done, edit their title, and remove them.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n\n- `todo done <id>` sets that todo's `done` flag and persists it; a subsequent `todo list` shows it as done.\n- `todo done <id>` on an already-done todo is an idempotent no-op that still exits 0.\n- `todo edit <id> \"<title>\"` replaces that todo's title under the same non-empty validation as `add`, and persists the change.\n- `todo remove <id>` deletes that todo; a subsequent `todo list` no longer shows it.\n- `done`, `edit` and `remove` given an id absent from the store each exit non-zero, name the unknown id, and leave the store unchanged (verified by reading the store before and after).\n- Each operation is backed by a pure function over `List[Todo]` (`(List[Todo], ...) => Either[<error>, List[Todo]]`) tested without touching the filesystem; the CLI layer only wires that function to the store read/write.\n\n## M7: Add colors (Status: PENDING)\n\nColorize the table without breaking non-terminal output.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n\n- On a color-capable terminal, done and pending rows render with visibly different ANSI colors in the output string.\n- Setting `NO_COLOR` (per no-color.org) suppresses all ANSI codes — asserted by checking the rendered string contains no escape sequences.\n- When stdout is not a TTY (e.g. piped to a file), ANSI codes are suppressed by default, same as the `NO_COLOR` case.\n- The color-enabled decision is a pure function of an explicit boolean computed once at the edge (from env and TTY check) — the coloring function itself is tested by passing `true`/`false` directly, no real terminal or env mutation needed.\n- The M5 column-alignment tests still pass with color disabled: stripping ANSI codes from a colored row yields the same text as the uncolored row.\n\n## M8: Add YAML output and a switch argument (Status: PENDING)\n\nLet `list` render as YAML in addition to the colored table, selected by an explicit flag.\n","numLines":120,"startLine":140,"totalLines":267}}}
