{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019Rz6rrLtx1SL1boE9WgHB1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# SPEC v1 — Todo List CLI MVP\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tA single-binary CLI todo list, grown milestone by milestone so `just check` is green from the first commit. Domain logic is a pure core over `List[Todo]`; Cats Effect sits at the edge for the filesystem and stdout. The store is one YAML file. The same data is reachable two ways directly from the CLI: a colored, aligned table for a human at a terminal, and raw YAML for a script or another tool.\n6\t\n7\t## Scope\n8\t\n9\t- One user, one machine, one YAML file as the store.\n10\t- Commands: `add`, `list`, `done`, `edit`, `remove`.\n11\t- Two renderers for `list`: a colored table (default) and YAML.\n12\t- A CI workflow that runs the same `just check` gate on every push and PR.\n13\t\n14\t## Non-goals (later specs)\n15\t\n16\t- A CLI-argument-parsing library — arguments are parsed by hand for now.\n17\t- Config files or environment-variable precedence for defaults.\n18\t- Filtering, sorting, priorities, due dates or tags.\n19\t- Any store other than a local YAML file (no SQLite, no remote/S3).\n20\t\n21\t## Architecture sketch\n22\t\n23\t```mermaid\n24\tflowchart LR\n25\t    CLI[CLI args] --> Core\n26\t    Store[(todos.yaml)] -- read --> Core\n27\t    Core -- pure List~Todo~ ops --> Core\n28\t    Core -- write --> Store\n29\t    Core --> TableR[Table renderer]\n30\t    Core --> YamlR[YAML renderer]\n31\t    TableR --> Stdout\n32\t    YamlR --> Stdout\n33\t```\n34\t\n35\tThe core (`Todo`, smart constructors, and the `add`/`done`/`edit`/`remove`\n36\toperations over `List[Todo]`) takes and returns values only. Reading the\n37\tYAML file, writing it back, deciding whether color is enabled, and printing\n38\tto stdout are the only things that touch `IO`.\n39\t\n40\t## M1: Scala and sbt setup (Status: ✅ DONE)\n41\t\n42\tEstablish the build so `just check` is the one CI gate before any domain\n43\tcode exists.\n44\t\n45\t**Acceptance Criteria:**\n46\t\n47\t- [x] `build.sbt` targets Scala 3 and declares `cats-core`, `cats-effect`, and, in `Test` scope, `munit`, `munit-cats-effect` and `munit-scalacheck`, per `docs/rules/WOW.md`.\n48\t- [x] `sbt compile` succeeds with `-Werror` in `scalacOptions` and zero warnings on a stock scaffold.\n49\t- [x] `sbt test` exits 0 with zero tests collected (no test sources yet).\n50\t- [x] A checked-in `.scalafmt.conf` makes `sbt scalafmtCheckAll` pass.\n51\t- [x] `just check` runs scalafmt-check, compile and test in one invocation and exits 0 on a clean checkout.\n52\t- [x] Sources live under `src/main/scala`, tests under `src/test/scala`.\n53\t- [x] `.github/workflows/ci.yml` (or equivalent) runs `just check` on `push` and `pull_request`, so a red gate locally is red in CI.\n54\t\n55\t**Implementation Details:**\n56\t\n57\t`build.sbt` sets `scalaVersion := \"3.3.4\"` (the\n58\tcurrent LTS) and adds `cats-core` 2.12.0, `cats-effect` 3.5.7, and, in\n59\t`Test` scope, `munit` 1.0.3, `munit-cats-effect` 2.0.0 and\n60\t`munit-scalacheck` 1.0.0, matching `docs/rules/WOW.md`. `scalacOptions`\n61\tincludes `-deprecation`, `-feature`, `-unchecked` and `-Werror`.\n62\t`project/plugins.sbt` adds `sbt-scalafmt` 2.5.2 so `sbt scalafmtCheckAll`\n63\tis a real sbt task, and `project/build.properties` pins `sbt.version` to\n64\t1.10.7 (the nix-provided `sbt` binary is a self-updating launcher that\n65\tfetches this version on first run). `.scalafmt.conf` targets the\n66\t`scala3` dialect. `src/main/scala` and `src/test/scala` exist as the\n67\tsource roots but are empty — there is no domain code yet, so `sbt\n68\tcompile` has zero sources/warnings and `sbt test` collects zero tests,\n69\tboth exiting 0. `.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs `just check` on `push`\n70\tand `pull_request` using `actions/setup-java`, `sbt/setup-sbt` and\n71\t`extractions/setup-just`. Verified locally: `just check`, `sbt compile`,\n72\t`sbt test` and `sbt scalafmtCheckAll` all exit 0 with zero warnings and\n73\tzero tests collected.\n74\t\n75\t## M2: Cats hello world (Status: IMPLEMENTED)\n76\t\n77\tProve the Cats Effect edge end to end before any domain logic.\n78\t\n79\t**Acceptance Criteria:**\n80\t\n81\t- [x] The entry point extends `IOApp` (or `IOApp.Simple`) — no `App`, no top-level `main` running side effects directly.\n82\t- [x] `sbt run` prints a greeting to stdout and exits 0.\n83\t- [x] The greeting text comes from a pure function with no `IO` in its signature; a test calls it directly and asserts on the returned string, no I/O involved.\n84\t- [x] A `munit.CatsEffectSuite` test runs the `IO` that prints the greeting and asserts on its result.\n85\t\n86\t**Implementation Details:**\n87\t\n88\t`todo.Main` extends `IOApp`; its `run` only\n89\tdelegates to `todo.Cli.run`. `todo.Greeting.message` is the pure greeting\n90\tstring, tested directly with no `IO`. `todo.Cli.greet(out: PrintStream): IO[Unit]`\n91\tis the effectful side — it takes the output stream as an explicit\n92\tparameter rather than writing to `System.out` directly, so the\n93\t`munit.CatsEffectSuite` test in `CliSuite` runs the real `IO`, captures\n94\twhat it printed to a `ByteArrayOutputStream`-backed `PrintStream`, and\n95\tasserts on that, with no mocking and no global state. `Main` calls\n96\t`Cli.greet(System.out)` at the edge. With no arguments, `Cli.run` treats\n97\tthe command as `Command.Greet`, so `sbt run` (no args) prints the\n98\tgreeting and exits 0 — the M3 subcommands (`add`, `list`) are added\n99\talongside it, and any other input reports \"Unknown command\" and exits\n100\tnon-zero.\n101\t\n102\t## M3: Create and read todos (Status: IMPLEMENTED)\n103\t\n104\tThe pure domain model and the YAML-backed store, with `add` and `list`.\n105\t\n106\t**Acceptance Criteria:**\n107\t\n108\t- [x] `Todo` holds an id, a non-empty title and a `done` flag; the title's smart constructor returns `Either[<error>, Todo]` for a blank or whitespace-only title — never throws, never silently trims to empty.\n109\t- [x] The store's read and write functions take the YAML file path as an explicit parameter — no hardcoded global path — so tests read and write a real temp file with no mocking.\n110\t- [x] Reading a store path that does not exist yields an empty todo list, not an error.\n111\t- [x] Writing a list of todos to the store and reading it back yields an equal list (round-trip test).\n112\t- [x] Reading a malformed YAML store file fails fast with a descriptive error on the `IO`/`Either` error channel, not a partial result or a raw stack trace.\n113\t- [x] `todo add \"<title>\"` appends one `Todo` with `done = false` to the store and prints confirmation naming the new id.\n114\t- [x] `todo add \"\"` (or an all-whitespace title) leaves the store untouched, exits non-zero, and names the problem.\n115\t- [x] `todo list` on an empty store prints a \"no todos\" message and exits 0.\n116\t- [x] `todo list` after two `add`s prints both todos, each with its id, title and done state.\n117\t\n118\t**Implementation Details:**\n119\t\n120\t`todo.core.Title` is an opaque type over\n121\t`String`; its only constructor, `Title.apply`, returns\n122\t`Either[Title.Error, Title]` and rejects a blank or whitespace-only raw\n123\tstring without ever trimming it into a silently-accepted empty value.\n124\t`todo.core.TodoId` is an opaque `Int`. `todo.core.Todo` is a plain case\n125\tclass of `(TodoId, Title, Boolean)` — it needs no smart constructor of\n126\tits own because both of its interesting fields are already\n127\tvalid-by-construction. `todo.core.Todos.add` is the pure operation:\n128\tgiven the existing list and a raw title, it validates the title, picks\n129\tthe next id as one past the current maximum (or `1` for an empty list),\n130\tand returns `Either[Todos.Error, (Todo, List[Todo])]` — the created\n131\t`Todo` and the appended list — with no `IO` in its signature.\n132\t\n133\tThe store uses `org.virtuslab:scala-yaml` (a dependency-free, Scala-3-native\n134\tYAML library) added to `build.sbt`. Rather than deriving a codec for\n135\t`Todo` directly — which would let the library's `Mirror`-based derivation\n136\tconstruct a `Todo` straight through `fromProduct`, bypassing `Title`'s\n137\tsmart constructor entirely — `todo.store.TodoRecord` is a separate,\n138\tprivate wire-format case class (`id: Int, title: String, done: Boolean\n139\tderives YamlCodec`). `todo.store.Store.read`/`write` take the file `Path`\n140\tas an explicit parameter (no global path) and convert between\n141\t`TodoRecord` and the domain `Todo`, so a record with a blank title read\n142\tfrom disk still fails through `Title.apply` rather than silently\n143\tproducing an invalid `Todo`. `read` checks existence first and returns\n144\t`Right(Nil)` for a missing file without touching the filesystem further;\n145\ta present file is parsed with `.as[List[TodoRecord]]`, and both a\n146\t`YamlError` (malformed syntax/shape) and an invalid record surface as a\n147\t`StoreError` with a human-readable `.message` — never a partial result,\n148\tnever a raw stack trace. One real bug surfaced by writing the round-trip\n149\ttest *before* the fix (per the TDD requirement): `org.virtuslab::scala-yaml` renders an\n150\tempty `Seq` as the empty string, which then fails to reparse\n151\t(`ComposerError: Expected YAML node, but found: StreamEnd`); `write`\n152\tspecial-cases an empty todo list to the literal `\"[]\\n\"` so an emptied\n153\tstore still round-trips.\n154\t\n155\t`todo.render.Renderer.renderList: List[Todo] => String` is the M3 plain\n156\tlisting (`No todos yet.` when empty, one `#<id> [pending|done] <title>`\n157\tline per todo otherwise) — a pure function with no `IO`, which M5 will\n158\treplace with a table.\n159\t\n160\t`todo.Cli` wires it together: `Cli.run(args, storePath, out)` takes the\n161\tstore path and output stream as explicit parameters (`Main` supplies the\n162\treal `todos.yaml` in the working directory and `System.out`; tests\n163\tsupply a temp file and a captured stream), so the CLI is exercised\n164\tend-to-end with a real fixture file and no process spawn or mocking.\n165\t`add` reads the store, calls `Todos.add`, and only writes back on\n166\tsuccess; a blank title never reaches `Store.write`, leaving the store\n167\tuntouched, and exits with `ExitCode.Error` naming the problem. A\n168\tsuccessful `add` writes the updated list and prints\n169\t`Added todo #<id>: <title>`. `list` reads the store and prints\n170\t`Renderer.renderList`.\n171\t\n172\t## M4: scalafmt quality gate as a pre-commit hook (Status: IMPLEMENTED)\n173\t\n174\tStop unformatted code from ever reaching a commit, not just from passing CI after the fact.\n175\t\n176\t**Acceptance Criteria:**\n177\t\n178\t- [x] A versioned git hook script under `.githooks/` runs `just check` (scalafmt-check, compile, test) before a commit is created.\n179\t- [x] `just install-hooks` points git at `.githooks` (`core.hooksPath`) and makes the hook executable, so one command per clone is enough to enable it.\n180\t- [x] Committing a change with a scalafmt violation is rejected by the hook (non-zero exit, commit not created) until the file is reformatted or `sbt scalafmtAll` is run.\n181\t- [x] A correctly formatted change commits cleanly through the hook with no manual `just check` step.\n182\t- [x] `CLAUDE.md` (or equivalent onboarding doc) documents running `just install-hooks` once per clone.\n183\t\n184\t**Implementation Details:**\n185\t\n186\tThe hook infrastructure this milestone\n187\tdescribes — `.githooks/pre-commit` (a `set -euo pipefail` script whose\n188\tonly job is `just check`) and the `install-hooks` recipe in `justfile`\n189\t(`git config core.hooksPath .githooks` then `chmod +x\n190\t.githooks/pre-commit`) — already existed in the working tree before this\n191\tmilestone was implemented, and `CLAUDE.md` already documented running\n192\t`just install-hooks` once per clone under its Checks section. `just\n193\tcheck` itself (`sbt -batch scalafmtCheckAll test`, plus the line/test/warning\n194\tsummary) also predates this milestone. No source changed for M4; the\n195\twork here was verifying, end to end in a disposable clone (`git clone`\n196\tto `/tmp`, so the real repo's hook state was never touched), that the\n197\texisting pieces actually satisfy every acceptance criterion rather than\n198\ttaking the prior commits' intent on faith:\n199\t`just install-hooks` sets `core.hooksPath` and makes the script\n200\texecutable; introducing a scalafmt violation (`val message:String=...`\n201\twith no spaces) and running `git commit` fails with scalafmt's `isn't\n202\tformatted properly!` warning, a non-zero exit from `just check`, and no\n203\tnew commit (`git log` unchanged); running `sbt scalafmtAll` to reformat\n204\tand committing a genuinely different, correctly formatted change then\n205\tsucceeds with no manual `just check` step, running the full check (20\n206\ttests, 0 warnings) as part of the commit itself. This is a\n207\tprocess/tooling criterion rather than a domain-logic one, so its \"test\"\n208\tis the shell transcript above rather than a `munit` suite — there is no\n209\tpure function to unit-test here, only a git/filesystem integration that\n210\t`docs/rules/WOW.md`'s \"no mocks\" rule would have us exercise for real\n211\texactly as done.\n212\t\n213\t## M5: List as table (Status: PENDING)\n214\t\n215\tTurn the plain listing from M3 into an aligned table.\n216\t\n217\t**Acceptance Criteria:**\n218\t\n219\t- [ ] `todo list` output starts with a header row naming the columns, then one row per todo.\n220\t- [ ] Columns stay aligned when values differ in width (a 1-digit vs 3-digit id, a short vs long title) — checked by asserting on the rendered string.\n221\t- [ ] The done/pending state renders as a distinct marker per row (e.g. `[x]`/`[ ]`), not a raw boolean.\n222\t- [ ] The empty-store case still renders the M3 \"no todos\" message, not a header with zero rows.\n223\t- [ ] The renderer is a pure function `List[Todo] => String`, tested directly with no I/O.\n224\t\n225\t## M6: Updates (Status: PENDING)\n226\t\n227\tMark todos done, edit their title, and remove them.\n228\t\n229\t**Acceptance Criteria:**\n230\t\n231\t- [ ] `todo done <id>` sets that todo's `done` flag and persists it; a subsequent `todo list` shows it as done.\n232\t- [ ] `todo done <id>` on an already-done todo is an idempotent no-op that still exits 0.\n233\t- [ ] `todo edit <id> \"<title>\"` replaces that todo's title under the same non-empty validation as `add`, and persists the change.\n234\t- [ ] `todo remove <id>` deletes that todo; a subsequent `todo list` no longer shows it.\n235\t- [ ] `done`, `edit` and `remove` given an id absent from the store each exit non-zero, name the unknown id, and leave the store unchanged (verified by reading the store before and after).\n236\t- [ ] Each operation is backed by a pure function over `List[Todo]` (`(List[Todo], ...) => Either[<error>, List[Todo]]`) tested without touching the filesystem; the CLI layer only wires that function to the store read/write.\n237\t\n238\t## M7: Add colors (Status: PENDING)\n239\t\n240\tColorize the table without breaking non-terminal output.\n241\t\n242\t**Acceptance Criteria:**\n243\t\n244\t- [ ] On a color-capable terminal, done and pending rows render with visibly different ANSI colors in the output string.\n245\t- [ ] Setting `NO_COLOR` (per no-color.org) suppresses all ANSI codes — asserted by checking the rendered string contains no escape sequences.\n246\t- [ ] When stdout is not a TTY (e.g. piped to a file), ANSI codes are suppressed by default, same as the `NO_COLOR` case.\n247\t- [ ] The color-enabled decision is a pure function of an explicit boolean computed once at the edge (from env and TTY check) — the coloring function itself is tested by passing `true`/`false` directly, no real terminal or env mutation needed.\n248\t- [ ] The M5 column-alignment tests still pass with color disabled: stripping ANSI codes from a colored row yields the same text as the uncolored row.\n249\t\n250\t## M8: Add YAML output and a switch argument (Status: PENDING)\n251\t\n252\tLet `list` render as YAML in addition to the colored table, selected by an explicit flag.\n253\t\n254\t**Acceptance Criteria:**\n255\t\n256\t- [ ] `todo list` and `todo list --output=table` both render the M7 colored table; `table` is the default when the flag is omitted.\n257\t- [ ] `todo list --output=yaml` renders the current todos as YAML using the same schema as the store file, such that feeding that output back through the store's YAML reader reconstructs an equal list of todos.\n258\t- [ ] `todo list --output=<anything else>` fails fast before rendering: a non-zero exit and a message naming the invalid value and the valid choices.\n259\t- [ ] The YAML renderer is a pure function `List[Todo] => String`, covered by a round-trip test independent of the table renderer.\n260\t- [ ] Empty-store output stays renderer-specific: `--output=table` still prints the \"no todos\" message; `--output=yaml` prints an empty YAML list (e.g. `[]`), not the human-readable message.\n261\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c107c929-b48e-44c9-89f6-008ba03025f5","uuid":"49ee753f-f941-4780-a10a-defabbf877eb","timestamp":"2026-09-30T01:22:10.855Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/isaiasbartelborth/Projects/hivemind/todo-list-cli/develop/docs/specs/SPEC.v1-todo-list-cli-mvp.md","content":"# SPEC v1 — Todo List CLI MVP\n\n## Goal\n\nA single-binary CLI todo list, grown milestone by milestone so `just check` is green from the first commit. Domain logic is a pure core over `List[Todo]`; Cats Effect sits at the edge for the filesystem and stdout. The store is one YAML file. The same data is reachable two ways directly from the CLI: a colored, aligned table for a human at a terminal, and raw YAML for a script or another tool.\n\n## Scope\n\n- One user, one machine, one YAML file as the store.\n- Commands: `add`, `list`, `done`, `edit`, `remove`.\n- Two renderers for `list`: a colored table (default) and YAML.\n- A CI workflow that runs the same `just check` gate on every push and PR.\n\n## Non-goals (later specs)\n\n- A CLI-argument-parsing library — arguments are parsed by hand for now.\n- Config files or environment-variable precedence for defaults.\n- Filtering, sorting, priorities, due dates or tags.\n- Any store other than a local YAML file (no SQLite, no remote/S3).\n\n## Architecture sketch\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    CLI[CLI args] --> Core\n    Store[(todos.yaml)] -- read --> Core\n    Core -- pure List~Todo~ ops --> Core\n    Core -- write --> Store\n    Core --> TableR[Table renderer]\n    Core --> YamlR[YAML renderer]\n    TableR --> Stdout\n    YamlR --> Stdout\n```\n\nThe core (`Todo`, smart constructors, and the `add`/`done`/`edit`/`remove`\noperations over `List[Todo]`) takes and returns values only. Reading the\nYAML file, writing it back, deciding whether color is enabled, and printing\nto stdout are the only things that touch `IO`.\n\n## M1: Scala and sbt setup (Status: ✅ DONE)\n\nEstablish the build so `just check` is the one CI gate before any domain\ncode exists.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n\n- [x] `build.sbt` targets Scala 3 and declares `cats-core`, `cats-effect`, and, in `Test` scope, `munit`, `munit-cats-effect` and `munit-scalacheck`, per `docs/rules/WOW.md`.\n- [x] `sbt compile` succeeds with `-Werror` in `scalacOptions` and zero warnings on a stock scaffold.\n- [x] `sbt test` exits 0 with zero tests collected (no test sources yet).\n- [x] A checked-in `.scalafmt.conf` makes `sbt scalafmtCheckAll` pass.\n- [x] `just check` runs scalafmt-check, compile and test in one invocation and exits 0 on a clean checkout.\n- [x] Sources live under `src/main/scala`, tests under `src/test/scala`.\n- [x] `.github/workflows/ci.yml` (or equivalent) runs `just check` on `push` and `pull_request`, so a red gate locally is red in CI.\n\n**Implementation Details:**\n\n`build.sbt` sets `scalaVersion := \"3.3.4\"` (the\ncurrent LTS) and adds `cats-core` 2.12.0, `cats-effect` 3.5.7, and, in\n`Test` scope, `munit` 1.0.3, `munit-cats-effect` 2.0.0 and\n`munit-scalacheck` 1.0.0, matching `docs/rules/WOW.md`. `scalacOptions`\nincludes `-deprecation`, `-feature`, `-unchecked` and `-Werror`.\n`project/plugins.sbt` adds `sbt-scalafmt` 2.5.2 so `sbt scalafmtCheckAll`\nis a real sbt task, and `project/build.properties` pins `sbt.version` to\n1.10.7 (the nix-provided `sbt` binary is a self-updating launcher that\nfetches this version on first run). `.scalafmt.conf` targets the\n`scala3` dialect. `src/main/scala` and `src/test/scala` exist as the\nsource roots but are empty — there is no domain code yet, so `sbt\ncompile` has zero sources/warnings and `sbt test` collects zero tests,\nboth exiting 0. `.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs `just check` on `push`\nand `pull_request` using `actions/setup-java`, `sbt/setup-sbt` and\n`extractions/setup-just`. Verified locally: `just check`, `sbt compile`,\n`sbt test` and `sbt scalafmtCheckAll` all exit 0 with zero warnings and\nzero tests collected.\n\n## M2: Cats hello world (Status: IMPLEMENTED)\n\nProve the Cats Effect edge end to end before any domain logic.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n\n- [x] The entry point extends `IOApp` (or `IOApp.Simple`) — no `App`, no top-level `main` running side effects directly.\n- [x] `sbt run` prints a greeting to stdout and exits 0.\n- [x] The greeting text comes from a pure function with no `IO` in its signature; a test calls it directly and asserts on the returned string, no I/O involved.\n- [x] A `munit.CatsEffectSuite` test runs the `IO` that prints the greeting and asserts on its result.\n\n**Implementation Details:**\n\n`todo.Main` extends `IOApp`; its `run` only\ndelegates to `todo.Cli.run`. `todo.Greeting.message` is the pure greeting\nstring, tested directly with no `IO`. `todo.Cli.greet(out: PrintStream): IO[Unit]`\nis the effectful side — it takes the output stream as an explicit\nparameter rather than writing to `System.out` directly, so the\n`munit.CatsEffectSuite` test in `CliSuite` runs the real `IO`, captures\nwhat it printed to a `ByteArrayOutputStream`-backed `PrintStream`, and\nasserts on that, with no mocking and no global state. `Main` calls\n`Cli.greet(System.out)` at the edge. With no arguments, `Cli.run` treats\nthe command as `Command.Greet`, so `sbt run` (no args) prints the\ngreeting and exits 0 — the M3 subcommands (`add`, `list`) are added\nalongside it, and any other input reports \"Unknown command\" and exits\nnon-zero.\n\n## M3: Create and read todos (Status: IMPLEMENTED)\n\nThe pure domain model and the YAML-backed store, with `add` and `list`.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n\n- [x] `Todo` holds an id, a non-empty title and a `done` flag; the title's smart constructor returns `Either[<error>, Todo]` for a blank or whitespace-only title — never throws, never silently trims to empty.\n- [x] The store's read and write functions take the YAML file path as an explicit parameter — no hardcoded global path — so tests read and write a real temp file with no mocking.\n- [x] Reading a store path that does not exist yields an empty todo list, not an error.\n- [x] Writing a list of todos to the store and reading it back yields an equal list (round-trip test).\n- [x] Reading a malformed YAML store file fails fast with a descriptive error on the `IO`/`Either` error channel, not a partial result or a raw stack trace.\n- [x] `todo add \"<title>\"` appends one `Todo` with `done = false` to the store and prints confirmation naming the new id.\n- [x] `todo add \"\"` (or an all-whitespace title) leaves the store untouched, exits non-zero, and names the problem.\n- [x] `todo list` on an empty store prints a \"no todos\" message and exits 0.\n- [x] `todo list` after two `add`s prints both todos, each with its id, title and done state.\n\n**Implementation Details:**\n\n`todo.core.Title` is an opaque type over\n`String`; its only constructor, `Title.apply`, returns\n`Either[Title.Error, Title]` and rejects a blank or whitespace-only raw\nstring without ever trimming it into a silently-accepted empty value.\n`todo.core.TodoId` is an opaque `Int`. `todo.core.Todo` is a plain case\nclass of `(TodoId, Title, Boolean)` — it needs no smart constructor of\nits own because both of its interesting fields are already\nvalid-by-construction. `todo.core.Todos.add` is the pure operation:\ngiven the existing list and a raw title, it validates the title, picks\nthe next id as one past the current maximum (or `1` for an empty list),\nand returns `Either[Todos.Error, (Todo, List[Todo])]` — the created\n`Todo` and the appended list — with no `IO` in its signature.\n\nThe store uses `org.virtuslab:scala-yaml` (a dependency-free, Scala-3-native\nYAML library) added to `build.sbt`. Rather than deriving a codec for\n`Todo` directly — which would let the library's `Mirror`-based derivation\nconstruct a `Todo` straight through `fromProduct`, bypassing `Title`'s\nsmart constructor entirely — `todo.store.TodoRecord` is a separate,\nprivate wire-format case class (`id: Int, title: String, done: Boolean\nderives YamlCodec`). `todo.store.Store.read`/`write` take the file `Path`\nas an explicit parameter (no global path) and convert between\n`TodoRecord` and the domain `Todo`, so a record with a blank title read\nfrom disk still fails through `Title.apply` rather than silently\nproducing an invalid `Todo`. `read` checks existence first and returns\n`Right(Nil)` for a missing file without touching the filesystem further;\na present file is parsed with `.as[List[TodoRecord]]`, and both a\n`YamlError` (malformed syntax/shape) and an invalid record surface as a\n`StoreError` with a human-readable `.message` — never a partial result,\nnever a raw stack trace. One real bug surfaced by writing the round-trip\ntest *before* the fix (per the TDD requirement): `org.virtuslab::scala-yaml` renders an\nempty `Seq` as the empty string, which then fails to reparse\n(`ComposerError: Expected YAML node, but found: StreamEnd`); `write`\nspecial-cases an empty todo list to the literal `\"[]\\n\"` so an emptied\nstore still round-trips.\n\n`todo.render.Renderer.renderList: List[Todo] => String` is the M3 plain\nlisting (`No todos yet.` when empty, one `#<id> [pending|done] <title>`\nline per todo otherwise) — a pure function with no `IO`, which M5 will\nreplace with a table.\n\n`todo.Cli` wires it together: `Cli.run(args, storePath, out)` takes the\nstore path and output stream as explicit parameters (`Main` supplies the\nreal `todos.yaml` in the working directory and `System.out`; tests\nsupply a temp file and a captured stream), so the CLI is exercised\nend-to-end with a real fixture file and no process spawn or mocking.\n`add` reads the store, calls `Todos.add`, and only writes back on\nsuccess; a blank title never reaches `Store.write`, leaving the store\nuntouched, and exits with `ExitCode.Error` naming the problem. A\nsuccessful `add` writes the updated list and prints\n`Added todo #<id>: <title>`. `list` reads the store and prints\n`Renderer.renderList`.\n\n## M4: scalafmt quality gate as a pre-commit hook (Status: IMPLEMENTED)\n\nStop unformatted code from ever reaching a commit, not just from passing CI after the fact.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n\n- [x] A versioned git hook script under `.githooks/` runs `just check` (scalafmt-check, compile, test) before a commit is created.\n- [x] `just install-hooks` points git at `.githooks` (`core.hooksPath`) and makes the hook executable, so one command per clone is enough to enable it.\n- [x] Committing a change with a scalafmt violation is rejected by the hook (non-zero exit, commit not created) until the file is reformatted or `sbt scalafmtAll` is run.\n- [x] A correctly formatted change commits cleanly through the hook with no manual `just check` step.\n- [x] `CLAUDE.md` (or equivalent onboarding doc) documents running `just install-hooks` once per clone.\n\n**Implementation Details:**\n\nThe hook infrastructure this milestone\ndescribes — `.githooks/pre-commit` (a `set -euo pipefail` script whose\nonly job is `just check`) and the `install-hooks` recipe in `justfile`\n(`git config core.hooksPath .githooks` then `chmod +x\n.githooks/pre-commit`) — already existed in the working tree before this\nmilestone was implemented, and `CLAUDE.md` already documented running\n`just install-hooks` once per clone under its Checks section. `just\ncheck` itself (`sbt -batch scalafmtCheckAll test`, plus the line/test/warning\nsummary) also predates this milestone. No source changed for M4; the\nwork here was verifying, end to end in a disposable clone (`git clone`\nto `/tmp`, so the real repo's hook state was never touched), that the\nexisting pieces actually satisfy every acceptance criterion rather than\ntaking the prior commits' intent on faith:\n`just install-hooks` sets `core.hooksPath` and makes the script\nexecutable; introducing a scalafmt violation (`val message:String=...`\nwith no spaces) and running `git commit` fails with scalafmt's `isn't\nformatted properly!` warning, a non-zero exit from `just check`, and no\nnew commit (`git log` unchanged); running `sbt scalafmtAll` to reformat\nand committing a genuinely different, correctly formatted change then\nsucceeds with no manual `just check` step, running the full check (20\ntests, 0 warnings) as part of the commit itself. This is a\nprocess/tooling criterion rather than a domain-logic one, so its \"test\"\nis the shell transcript above rather than a `munit` suite — there is no\npure function to unit-test here, only a git/filesystem integration that\n`docs/rules/WOW.md`'s \"no mocks\" rule would have us exercise for real\nexactly as done.\n\n## M5: List as table (Status: PENDING)\n\nTurn the plain listing from M3 into an aligned table.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n\n- [ ] `todo list` output starts with a header row naming the columns, then one row per todo.\n- [ ] Columns stay aligned when values differ in width (a 1-digit vs 3-digit id, a short vs long title) — checked by asserting on the rendered string.\n- [ ] The done/pending state renders as a distinct marker per row (e.g. `[x]`/`[ ]`), not a raw boolean.\n- [ ] The empty-store case still renders the M3 \"no todos\" message, not a header with zero rows.\n- [ ] The renderer is a pure function `List[Todo] => String`, tested directly with no I/O.\n\n## M6: Updates (Status: PENDING)\n\nMark todos done, edit their title, and remove them.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n\n- [ ] `todo done <id>` sets that todo's `done` flag and persists it; a subsequent `todo list` shows it as done.\n- [ ] `todo done <id>` on an already-done todo is an idempotent no-op that still exits 0.\n- [ ] `todo edit <id> \"<title>\"` replaces that todo's title under the same non-empty validation as `add`, and persists the change.\n- [ ] `todo remove <id>` deletes that todo; a subsequent `todo list` no longer shows it.\n- [ ] `done`, `edit` and `remove` given an id absent from the store each exit non-zero, name the unknown id, and leave the store unchanged (verified by reading the store before and after).\n- [ ] Each operation is backed by a pure function over `List[Todo]` (`(List[Todo], ...) => Either[<error>, List[Todo]]`) tested without touching the filesystem; the CLI layer only wires that function to the store read/write.\n\n## M7: Add colors (Status: PENDING)\n\nColorize the table without breaking non-terminal output.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n\n- [ ] On a color-capable terminal, done and pending rows render with visibly different ANSI colors in the output string.\n- [ ] Setting `NO_COLOR` (per no-color.org) suppresses all ANSI codes — asserted by checking the rendered string contains no escape sequences.\n- [ ] When stdout is not a TTY (e.g. piped to a file), ANSI codes are suppressed by default, same as the `NO_COLOR` case.\n- [ ] The color-enabled decision is a pure function of an explicit boolean computed once at the edge (from env and TTY check) — the coloring function itself is tested by passing `true`/`false` directly, no real terminal or env mutation needed.\n- [ ] The M5 column-alignment tests still pass with color disabled: stripping ANSI codes from a colored row yields the same text as the uncolored row.\n\n## M8: Add YAML output and a switch argument (Status: PENDING)\n\nLet `list` render as YAML in addition to the colored table, selected by an explicit flag.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n\n- [ ] `todo list` and `todo list --output=table` both render the M7 colored table; `table` is the default when the flag is omitted.\n- [ ] `todo list --output=yaml` renders the current todos as YAML using the same schema as the store file, such that feeding that output back through the store's YAML reader reconstructs an equal list of todos.\n- [ ] `todo list --output=<anything else>` fails fast before rendering: a non-zero exit and a message naming the invalid value and the valid choices.\n- [ ] The YAML renderer is a pure function `List[Todo] => String`, covered by a round-trip test independent of the table renderer.\n- [ ] Empty-store output stays renderer-specific: `--output=table` still prints the \"no todos\" message; `--output=yaml` prints an empty YAML list (e.g. `[]`), not the human-readable message.\n","numLines":261,"startLine":1,"totalLines":261}}}
